---
import ToolPageShell from '../../../components/ToolPageShell.astro';
import ChocolateToxicityCalc from '../../../components/calculators/ChocolateToxicityCalc.astro';
import EmergencyHotlines from '../../../components/EmergencyHotlines.astro';
import { SRC } from '../../../data/sources.ts';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'My dog ate chocolate. What should I do right now?',
    a: 'Do not wait for symptoms. Call your veterinarian, an emergency clinic, the <a href="tel:+18884264435">ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center at (888) 426-4435</a>, or <a href="tel:+18557647661">Pet Poison Helpline at (855) 764-7661</a> immediately. Consultation fees may apply. Have your dog’s weight, the type and amount of chocolate, and the time of ingestion ready.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I make my dog vomit at home?',
    a: 'Only if a veterinarian or poison control specialist tells you to. Inducing vomiting incorrectly can cause aspiration or other harm, and it is not appropriate in some situations. Never give salt or other home remedies.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is chocolate toxic to dogs?',
    a: 'Chocolate contains methylxanthines, mainly theobromine and caffeine. Dogs metabolize theobromine slowly, so it builds up and can affect the heart, nervous system, and kidneys.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which chocolate is the most dangerous?',
    a: 'Baking (unsweetened) chocolate, cocoa powder, and dark chocolate contain the most theobromine per ounce. Milk chocolate has less but can still be dangerous for small dogs or large amounts. White chocolate has very little theobromine but is fatty and sugary.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the symptoms of chocolate poisoning?',
    a: 'Vomiting, diarrhea, restlessness, increased thirst, panting, a fast or irregular heartbeat, tremors, and seizures. Signs usually start within 6 to 12 hours but can be delayed, and effects can last 24 hours or more.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a low result from this calculator safe?',
    a: 'Not necessarily. Theobromine content varies between products, and some dogs are more sensitive, especially those with heart disease. Other ingredients such as xylitol, raisins, or macadamia nuts can be dangerous too. When in doubt, call a vet or poison control.',
  },
];

const sources = [
  { ...SRC.aspcaPeopleFoods, accessed: '2026-10-20' },
  { ...SRC.petPoisonChocolate, accessed: '2026-10-20' },
  { ...SRC.msdChocolate, accessed: '2026-10-20' },
  {
    title: 'Pet poisoning and household hazards',
    url: 'https://www.avma.org/resources-tools/pet-owners/petcare/poisoning-pets',
    publisher: 'AVMA',
    accessed: '2026-10-20',
  },
];
---

<ToolPageShell
  slug="dog-chocolate-toxicity-calculator"
  title="Dog Ate Chocolate? Chocolate Toxicity Calculator"
  description="Estimate the theobromine dose from the chocolate type, amount and your dog's weight, and see the risk level. If your dog ate chocolate, call a vet now."
  h1="Dog chocolate toxicity calculator"
  answer="Call a vet or poison hotline now — then enter weight, chocolate type, and amount for a rough theobromine dose estimate. This is triage education, not a safe-dose tool."
  formulaSummary="Dose (mg/kg) ≈ (grams of chocolate × theobromine mg per gram) ÷ body weight kg. Approximate mg/g: white 0.01, milk 2, dark 5.5, baking 14. Risk bands: under 20, 20–40, 40–60, over 60 mg/kg."
  takeaways={[
    'Emergency first: call a vet or poison control before using any calculator.',
    'Dark and baking chocolate are far more dangerous than milk or white chocolate.',
    'Toxic risk depends on dose per kilogram, so small dogs are at higher risk.',
    'Signs may start 6–12 hours later; do not wait for symptoms.',
    'A low estimate is not a guarantee of safety.',
  ]}
  faq={faq}
  sources={sources}
  emergency
  adSlotId="tool-after-results-safety"
>
  <div slot="emergency">
    <p class="emergency-lead">
      Call a vet or poison hotline first — then estimate dose below.
    </p>
    <EmergencyHotlines />
  </div>

  <ChocolateToxicityCalc slot="calculator" />

  <h2>What to do first</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Remove any remaining chocolate and wrappers from your dog’s reach.</li>
    <li>
      Call your veterinarian, an emergency clinic, or a poison hotline immediately —{' '}
      <a href="tel:+18884264435">ASPCA APCC (888) 426-4435</a> or{' '}
      <a href="tel:+18557647661">Pet Poison Helpline (855) 764-7661</a>. Do not wait for symptoms.
    </li>
    <li>Gather your dog’s weight, the type and amount of chocolate eaten, and when it happened.</li>
    <li>Follow the professional’s instructions. Do not induce vomiting unless told to.</li>
    <li>Use this calculator only as a rough supplement while you are on the phone or traveling to the clinic.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why chocolate is toxic to dogs</h2>
  <p>
    Chocolate contains methylxanthines, mostly theobromine and a smaller amount of caffeine. Humans clear theobromine
    quickly, but dogs eliminate it slowly, so it accumulates and acts on the heart and nervous system. The risk depends
    on <em>dose per kilogram</em>, which is why the same chocolate bar is a minor event for a 40 kg dog and an emergency
    for a 3 kg dog.
  </p>

  <h2>How it&apos;s calculated</h2>
  <p>
    The tool multiplies grams of chocolate by an approximate theobromine content, then divides by your dog’s weight to
    get milligrams per kilogram. Mid-range figures: about 0.01 mg/g white, 2 milk, 5.5 dark, and 14 baking. Real products
    vary with cacao percentage. The result is matched to commonly cited veterinary risk bands:
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Approximate theobromine risk bands used for education</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Theobromine dose</th><th>What may happen</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Under 20 mg/kg</td><td>Often mild or no signs; monitor and call if symptoms appear.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>20–40 mg/kg</td><td>Vomiting, restlessness, increased heart rate possible.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>40–60 mg/kg</td><td>Moderate to severe signs, including cardiac effects.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Over 60 mg/kg</td><td>Seizures and life-threatening signs possible; emergency.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A 10 kg (22 lb) dog eats 100 g of milk chocolate (about a large bar). Theobromine ≈ 100 × 2 = 200 mg, so the dose is
    20 mg/kg, at the edge of the mild-to-moderate range. The same dog eating 100 g of dark chocolate gets 550 mg, or
    55 mg/kg, in the moderate-to-severe range — a clear emergency call. A 40 kg dog eating the same 100 g of milk chocolate
    receives 5 mg/kg (likely milder), but calling is still sensible.
  </p>

  <h2>Symptoms to watch for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Vomiting or diarrhea, often within a few hours.</li>
    <li>Restlessness, hyperactivity, excessive panting, or increased thirst and urination.</li>
    <li>Rapid, irregular, or racing heartbeat.</li>
    <li>Muscle tremors, stiffness, or seizures.</li>
    <li>Collapse, which is a life-threatening emergency.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limits of this calculator</h2>
  <p>
    The tool is a simplified education aid. It does not know the exact product, how much cacao it contains, whether your
    dog vomited, your dog’s health history, or when ingestion occurred. A low estimate does not mean no treatment is
    needed. Read the <a href="/guides/chocolate-and-dogs-safety/">chocolate and dogs safety guide</a> for prevention
    context (why/when/is it dangerous — not a second calculator).
  </p>
</ToolPageShell>
